.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt QUARK_EVENT_DUMP 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm quark_event_dump
.Nd dump a
.Vt quark_event
to a
.Vt FILE
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In quark.h
.Ft int
.Fn quark_event_dump "struct quark_event *qev" "FILE *file"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
dumps a human readable format of the event pointed to by
.Fa qev
to the file specified in
.Fa file .
.Sh OUTPUT EXAMPLE
.Bd -literal
->818621 (FORK+EXEC+EXIT)
  TAGS  tags=[ log ]
  COMM  comm=git
  CMDL  cmdline=[ /usr/bin/git, --no-pager, ls-tree, --name-only, -z, HEAD, --, quark_event_dump.3 ]
  PROC  ppid=5082
  PROC  uid=1000 gid=1000 suid=1000 sgid=1000 euid=1000 egid=1000
  PROC  cap_inheritable=0x0 cap_permitted=0x0 cap_effective=0x0
  PROC  cap_bset=0x1ffffffffff cap_ambient=0x0
  PROC  time_boot=1712219191035753014
  CWD   cwd=/home/haesbaert
  FILE  filename=/usr/bin/git
  EXIT  exit_code=0 exit_time=72031260061483
.Ed
.Sh FORMAT
The number
.Sy 818621
after the
.Sy ->
is the pid of the originating process.
The string
.Sy (FORK+EXEC+EXIT)
describes what is aggregated in
.Sy this
specific event.
The following fields contain all the data we know about pid
.Sy 818621 ,
which originates both from this event, and whatever has been learned before and
is cached.
.Pp
The first column of the subsequent lines is a description of the
.Vt flag
from where the fields in the second column originates from.
For example
.Sy PROC
is
.Dv QUARK_F_PROC
and produces the fields
.Sy ppid ,
.Sy uid ,
.Sy gid ,
.Sy suid ...
.Pp
.Sy TAGS
is not a flag, it lists the names of the tags that the tag blocks of the
ruleset set on this event, see
.Xr quark-mon 8 .
An event without a tag has no
.Sy TAGS
line.
.Pp
.Sy BLOC
is not a flag either.
It is printed for an operation that a
.Cm block
of the ruleset denied, a
.Sy (BLOCKED)
event:
.Bd -literal
->238271 (BLOCKED)
  TAGS  tags=[ blocked ]
  BLOC  op=rename block=1 pid=238271 tid=238271 comm=mv
  BLOC  path=/etc/passwd
  BLOC  path2=/tmp/p
  COMM  comm=mv
  ...
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy op
is what was denied:
.Sy fork ,
.Sy exec ,
.Sy create ,
.Sy chmod ,
.Sy open ,
.Sy truncate ,
.Sy rename ,
.Sy link
or
.Sy unlink .
It is what the kernel was asked to do, not the system call: an open that
would make a file is
.Sy create ,
and a program that opens a file for writing before it truncates it is denied
at the
.Sy open .
.Sy block
is the number of the block in the ruleset, from 0.
.Sy pid ,
.Sy tid
and
.Sy comm
are of the thread that tried.
.Sy path
is the executable for a fork and an exec, of the parent for a fork, and the
file otherwise.
.Sy path2
is the new path of a rename or a link.
The lines after these describe the process that tried, as it is in the cache.
With the NOVA backend alone that process may not be known, and only the
lines above are printed.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Zero on success, -1 in error from
.Xr fwrite 3 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr quark_process_lookup 3 ,
.Xr quark_queue_block 3 ,
.Xr quark_queue_close 3 ,
.Xr quark_queue_default_attr 3 ,
.Xr quark_queue_get_epollfd 3 ,
.Xr quark_queue_get_event 3 ,
.Xr quark_queue_get_stats 3 ,
.Xr quark_queue_open 3 ,
.Xr quark 7 ,
.Xr quark-btf 8 ,
.Xr quark-mon 8
